def register(T):
    # ===== SCIENCE =====

    T["how vision works in the human eye"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "the biological process by which the eye converts light into neural signals interpreted as images, involving the cornea, lens, retina, photoreceptors (rods and cones), and the optic nerve to the visual cortex",
        "how": "light enters the cornea (which does most of the focusing), passes through the pupil and adjustable lens, and lands on the retina. Photoreceptors transduce light into electrical signals: rods for low-light, cones for color and detail. Bipolar and ganglion cells process the signal locally; the optic nerve carries it to the lateral geniculate nucleus and then to the primary visual cortex, where edge, motion, and feature detectors construct perception",
        "why": "vision provides about 80 percent of human sensory information, and understanding it underlies treatment of cataracts, glaucoma, macular degeneration, and refractive errors. It also informs camera design, machine vision, and the study of how brains build representations from raw sensor data",
        "vs": "human vision differs from many animals: bees see ultraviolet, mantis shrimp distinguish 12 color channels, and predator birds resolve far finer detail. It differs from cameras in dynamic range (much wider than typical sensors) and in the active, predictive processing the brain layers on top of raw sensory input",
        "ex": "color blindness, affecting about 8 percent of men of European descent, results from missing or altered cone photopigment genes on the X chromosome. The most common form (deuteranomaly) shifts green-cone sensitivity, making certain reds and greens hard to distinguish but leaving overall vision functional",
        "mis": "people think the eye captures images like a camera. The retina samples a small high-resolution central region (fovea) and a much lower-resolution periphery; the brain stitches together a perceived stable scene through saccades and predictive processing. Another myth is that carrots dramatically improve vision; vitamin A is needed to make rhodopsin, but excess does not improve normal sight",
    }

    T["acid-base chemistry in everyday life"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "the branch of chemistry concerning compounds that donate protons (acids) or accept them (bases), with pH measuring acidity on a 0-14 logarithmic scale where 7 is neutral, lower is acidic, and higher is basic (alkaline)",
        "how": "in water, acids increase H+ (or H3O+) concentration and bases increase OH- concentration. Strong acids (HCl) dissociate fully; weak acids (acetic acid) partially. Buffers, mixtures of weak acid and conjugate base, resist pH changes by absorbing added H+ or OH-. Reactions between acids and bases produce water and a salt, often releasing heat",
        "why": "acid-base chemistry governs digestion (stomach acid at pH 1-2), blood physiology (tightly buffered at pH 7.35-7.45), soaps and detergents, baking (leavening), water treatment, antacids, and ocean acidification (a major effect of rising CO2). Most household chemistry can be understood through pH",
        "vs": "Bronsted-Lowry acid-base definitions (proton transfer) differ from Lewis (electron-pair acceptor/donor, more general) and Arrhenius (older, water-only). Acidity differs from corrosivity (which depends on the specific anion and material) and from sourness (a related but distinct sensory perception)",
        "ex": "baking soda (sodium bicarbonate, a base) reacts with vinegar (acetic acid) to produce carbon dioxide bubbles, water, and sodium acetate. The same reaction happens at a controlled rate inside cake batter when buttermilk and baking soda combine, leavening the cake without yeast",
        "mis": "people think 'acid' means dangerous. Lemon juice is more acidic than coffee; both are safe to drink. Another myth is that alkaline water has health benefits; the stomach quickly equalizes pH, and there is little evidence of systemic benefit",
    }

    T["how vaccines induce long-lasting immunity"] = {
        "_cat": "science",
        "what": "the immunological process by which a vaccine, presenting weakened or fragmentary pathogen components, primes the adaptive immune system to remember and rapidly respond to future infection by the real pathogen",
        "how": "the vaccine antigens are taken up by dendritic cells, which mature and present them to naive T and B cells in lymph nodes. Activated B cells produce antibodies and differentiate into long-lived plasma and memory B cells. Memory T cells also form. On later infection, memory cells respond within hours to days rather than the weeks naive responses require, often clearing the pathogen before symptoms develop",
        "why": "vaccines are the most cost-effective public-health intervention in history, eradicating smallpox, nearly eradicating polio, and dramatically reducing measles, rubella, and many other diseases. They underlie herd immunity, which protects those who cannot be vaccinated due to age or immune compromise",
        "vs": "vaccine-induced immunity differs from natural-infection immunity (similar mechanism, but vaccines avoid disease risk and often produce more uniform protection) and from passive immunity (transferred antibodies, fast but short-lived). Among vaccine platforms, live-attenuated, inactivated, subunit, mRNA, and viral-vector each trigger different immune responses",
        "ex": "the measles vaccine produces protection lasting decades from a two-dose childhood schedule, with about 97 percent effectiveness. Outbreaks in regions with declining vaccination coverage (such as several US states in recent years) demonstrate both the vaccine's effectiveness and the speed at which measles returns when herd immunity drops below roughly 95 percent",
        "mis": "people think vaccine immunity wanes quickly. Some vaccines do require boosters (tetanus, COVID-19), but many produce decades-long protection. Another myth is that vaccines overload the immune system; even infants encounter thousands of antigens daily from food and environment, far more than any vaccine schedule",
    }

    # ===== MATH =====

    T["set theory basics"] = {
        "_cat": "math",
        "what": "the branch of mathematics dealing with collections of objects called sets, the relations and operations on them (union, intersection, complement, subset), and the logical foundations these provide for the rest of mathematics",
        "how": "sets are defined by their elements, with order and repetition irrelevant. Union and intersection combine sets; complement excludes elements; Cartesian products build pairs. Russell's paradox (the set of all sets that do not contain themselves) led to axiomatic set theory like ZFC (Zermelo-Fraenkel with Choice), which restricts set formation to avoid contradictions",
        "why": "set theory provides the foundational language for mathematics: functions, relations, ordered pairs, numbers, and algebraic structures are all defined in terms of sets in standard treatments. It underlies modern logic, computer science (databases, type theory), probability, and topology",
        "vs": "set theory differs from category theory (which abstracts the relations between mathematical structures rather than building them from elements), from type theory (which assigns objects to types rather than memberships), and from naive collections (which set theory rigorously formalizes to avoid paradox)",
        "ex": "the power set of {a, b} (all subsets) has four elements: {}, {a}, {b}, {a, b}. Cantor's theorem proves that the power set of any set is strictly larger than the set itself, leading to a hierarchy of infinities (aleph-null, aleph-one, ...)",
        "mis": "people think sets are just collections. The empty set is a set, repetition does not matter, and very different formal axioms (ZFC, NBG, Morse-Kelley) handle the same intuitive idea slightly differently. Another myth is that all infinities are equal; Cantor's diagonalization shows that the real numbers are uncountably more numerous than the integers",
    }

    T["the dot product and its uses"] = {
        "_cat": "math",
        "what": "an algebraic operation taking two equal-length vectors and returning a scalar equal to the sum of products of corresponding entries, equivalently the product of their magnitudes times the cosine of the angle between them",
        "how": "for vectors u = (u1, ..., un) and v = (v1, ..., vn), u dot v = u1 v1 + ... + un vn. Geometrically, it equals |u| |v| cos(theta). The dot product is positive when vectors point similarly, zero when perpendicular, negative when opposing. It is bilinear, symmetric, and positive-definite when restricted to v dot v",
        "why": "the dot product is the workhorse of vector geometry: projection (the projection of u onto v has length u dot v / |v|), angle measurement, orthogonality tests, and the squared-distance formula. It appears in physics (work equals force dot displacement), graphics (lighting via surface-normal dot light-direction), and machine learning (cosine similarity for embeddings)",
        "vs": "the dot product differs from the cross product (returns a vector perpendicular to two 3D inputs, used for area and torque), from the outer product (returns a matrix), and from element-wise (Hadamard) products (returns a vector). It generalizes to inner products on abstract vector spaces, including function spaces with integrals",
        "ex": "two text documents represented as bag-of-words vectors have a cosine similarity equal to their dot product divided by the product of their lengths. Search and recommendation systems compute billions of such dot products to retrieve similar items, often using GPU-friendly matrix multiplications",
        "mis": "people confuse the dot product with multiplication. It collapses two vectors to a single number; the result is a scalar, not a vector. Another myth is that the dot product only makes sense in three dimensions; it is defined for any number of dimensions, including the infinite-dimensional inner products of Hilbert spaces",
    }

    T["expected value in probability"] = {
        "_cat": "math",
        "what": "the long-run average outcome of a random variable, computed as the sum (or integral) of each possible value weighted by its probability, often denoted E(X) and central to probability and decision theory",
        "how": "for a discrete random variable X with values x_i and probabilities p_i, E(X) = sum of p_i times x_i. For continuous variables, replace the sum with an integral over the probability density. Expectation is linear: E(X + Y) = E(X) + E(Y) regardless of dependence. The law of large numbers says sample averages converge to the expected value as sample size grows",
        "why": "expected value sets fair prices for bets and insurance, drives optimal decisions under uncertainty, underlies regression (which fits the conditional expectation of Y given X), provides the mean parameter of distributions, and is the basis for risk calculations in finance",
        "vs": "expected value differs from the most likely outcome (the mode), the middle outcome (the median), and from variance (a measure of spread around the mean). It differs from utility, which weights outcomes by subjective preference rather than nominal value, an important distinction in economic decision-making",
        "ex": "in roulette, betting one dollar on a single number pays 35 to 1 if it hits. The probability of hitting is 1/38 (for double-zero American roulette). Expected value of the bet is (35 times 1/38) plus (-1 times 37/38), or about negative 5.3 cents per dollar wagered, the house edge",
        "mis": "people equate expected value with the typical experience. A lottery ticket with positive expected value due to a giant jackpot is still almost certainly a loss. Another myth is that expected value tells you whether to take a bet; for non-repeated bets with large stakes, risk aversion (favoring lower-variance outcomes) can rationally override expected-value maximization",
    }

    T["modular arithmetic and clock math"] = {
        "_cat": "math",
        "what": "arithmetic where numbers wrap around upon reaching a fixed modulus, formalized as integers considered equivalent if they differ by a multiple of the modulus, written a equiv b (mod n)",
        "how": "addition, subtraction, and multiplication work as usual but results are reduced modulo n. Division is replaced by multiplication by a modular inverse, which exists for a (mod n) iff gcd(a, n) equals 1. The set Z/nZ is a ring, and a field when n is prime, providing rich algebraic structure",
        "why": "modular arithmetic underlies cryptography (RSA, Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve schemes), error-detection codes (ISBN check digits, IBAN check digits), pseudorandom-number generators, hash functions, and competitive-programming techniques. It is fundamental to number theory and discrete mathematics",
        "vs": "modular arithmetic differs from ordinary integer arithmetic in finiteness and in the existence of multiplicative inverses for some elements. It differs from rational arithmetic, where every nonzero element has an inverse. Composite moduli give rings that are not fields, with zero divisors",
        "ex": "RSA encryption raises a message m to the public exponent e modulo n (the product of two large primes), producing the ciphertext. Decryption raises the ciphertext to the private exponent d modulo n, recovering m. The security relies on factoring being hard while exponentiation modulo n is fast",
        "mis": "people think modular arithmetic is only for clocks. While 12-hour and 24-hour clocks use mod 12 and mod 24, the broader mathematical framework powers most modern cryptography. Another myth is that the modular inverse is the same as 1/a; for instance, 3 inverse mod 7 is 5 because 3 times 5 equals 15 equiv 1 (mod 7)",
    }

    # ===== TECHNOLOGY =====

    T["how database indexes speed up queries"] = {
        "_cat": "technology",
        "what": "auxiliary data structures (most commonly B+ trees, hash tables, or LSM-trees) that databases maintain alongside tables to allow rapid lookup of rows by key without scanning the entire table",
        "how": "a B+ tree index keeps keys in sorted order in a balanced tree, with leaves linked for range scans. Lookup descends the tree in O(log n) page reads. Hash indexes give O(1) point lookups but cannot range-scan. The query planner uses statistics to decide whether to use an index or scan the full table; selectivity, table size, and index cost matter",
        "why": "indexes turn a query that scans gigabytes into one that reads kilobytes. Without indexes, even simple lookups on a million-row table can take seconds; with the right index they take microseconds. Index design is one of the highest-leverage performance levers in database tuning",
        "vs": "B+ tree indexes differ from hash indexes (faster point lookup, no range scans) and from LSM-trees (write-optimized, common in NoSQL). Indexes differ from materialized views (which store query results) and from full-text indexes (which use posting lists for word search). Composite indexes on multiple columns differ from single-column indexes in which queries they accelerate",
        "ex": "adding an index on a 100-million-row users table's email column changes a SELECT WHERE email = ... query from a full-table scan (seconds) to an index lookup (milliseconds). The trade-off is slower writes (each insert/update must also update the index) and additional disk space",
        "mis": "people add indexes on every column and assume queries get faster. Each index slows writes and consumes space; redundant indexes hurt overall performance. Another myth is that the database always uses an index when one exists; if a query touches a large fraction of the table, a full scan can be faster than random index lookups",
    }

    T["how Git's commit graph works"] = {
        "_cat": "technology",
        "what": "the directed acyclic graph of commits that Git uses to represent the history of a repository, where each commit references its parent(s), a tree of files, an author, and a message, identified by a SHA-1 (or SHA-256) hash of its contents",
        "how": "every commit stores a snapshot of the repository (via a tree object pointing to blob objects for files) and the SHA hash of its parent commit (or two parents for merges). Hashes form a Merkle DAG: changing any file changes the tree, which changes the commit, which changes every later commit. Branches are simply named pointers to commits; checkout, merge, and rebase manipulate which commits are reachable from each branch",
        "why": "the commit graph makes Git distributed, integrity-checked, and efficient. Any clone has the full history. Tampering with old history changes hashes, making it detectable. Merge resolution uses common ancestors found by graph traversal. Tools like git log, git bisect, and git rebase all operate on this graph",
        "vs": "Git's commit graph differs from centralized VCS like Subversion (linear revision numbers, central server authority) and from snapshot-only filesystems (no parent tracking). It differs from a simple log file by its DAG structure (multiple branches and merges) and Merkle hashing (tamper-evident)",
        "ex": "a feature branch with three commits, merged back into main with a merge commit, produces a diamond-shaped subgraph: main's tip and the feature tip share an ancestor, and the merge commit has two parents. git log --graph visualizes this; rebase rewrites it into a linear sequence by replaying commits onto the tip",
        "mis": "people think a commit stores a diff. Git stores full snapshots of file contents, deduplicated by hash; diffs are computed on demand. Another myth is that branches are heavy objects; a branch is literally a 40-character file holding a commit hash, making branching practically free",
    }

    T["how containers differ from virtual machines"] = {
        "_cat": "technology",
        "what": "two technologies for isolating workloads on a single physical machine: containers share the host kernel and isolate at the OS level using namespaces and cgroups, while virtual machines run a full guest OS on virtualized hardware via a hypervisor",
        "how": "a container is a process tree running with separated views of mount points, networks, processes, users, and IPC, plus resource limits via cgroups. It uses the host kernel directly, starting in milliseconds with overhead near native. A VM, by contrast, runs a complete OS image atop a hypervisor that emulates or paravirtualizes hardware; boot takes seconds to minutes, with measurable but small CPU overhead and significant memory cost",
        "why": "the choice shapes deployment density, startup latency, security boundaries, and operational complexity. Containers enabled microservices and modern CI/CD by making deployments lightweight and reproducible. VMs remain essential where strong isolation is required (multi-tenant clouds, untrusted workloads) or where running different kernels is needed",
        "vs": "containers differ from VMs in kernel sharing, weight, and isolation guarantees. They differ from process isolation alone (no kernel sharing in VMs; weaker but real isolation in containers). Newer technologies like Firecracker microVMs and gVisor narrow the gap by combining VM isolation with container-like overhead, blurring the line",
        "ex": "AWS Lambda runs each function in a Firecracker microVM that boots in ~125 milliseconds and uses about 5 MiB of overhead, achieving VM-grade isolation with container-like density. Traditional VMs (EC2 instances) take seconds to minutes to boot and consume hundreds of MiB of overhead each",
        "mis": "people think containers are inherently insecure. With user namespaces, seccomp filters, AppArmor or SELinux profiles, and proper image hygiene, containers achieve solid isolation suitable for trusted multi-tenant environments. Another myth is that VMs are obsolete; for security-critical or kernel-level workloads, they remain the standard",
    }

    T["how SAT solvers work"] = {
        "_cat": "technology",
        "what": "programs that decide whether a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form has a satisfying assignment, using algorithmic techniques (DPLL with conflict-driven clause learning, CDCL) that solve real-world instances with millions of variables despite the NP-hard worst case",
        "how": "modern SAT solvers extend DPLL with: choosing a variable, propagating implied assignments via unit propagation, and backtracking on conflicts. CDCL adds learned clauses derived from analyzing each conflict, restarts to escape stuck regions, and activity-based variable heuristics (VSIDS) that focus search on recently useful variables. These ideas make practical performance vastly outperform worst-case bounds",
        "why": "SAT solvers underpin formal verification of hardware and software, cryptanalysis, planning in AI, dependency resolution in package managers, and combinatorial optimization. Modern microprocessor verification is unthinkable without SAT-based and SMT-based tools; bugs that would have shipped are caught in design phases",
        "vs": "SAT solvers differ from SMT solvers (which extend SAT to handle theories like arithmetic, arrays, bit-vectors), from constraint solvers (which work over richer domains), from brute-force enumeration (impossible at industrial scales), and from heuristic search (which gives no guarantee of completeness)",
        "ex": "Intel and AMD use SAT and SMT solvers extensively in chip verification, encoding correctness properties of arithmetic units and memory systems as Boolean formulas. The 1994 Pentium FDIV bug, which slipped past pre-SAT verification, shipped to millions of CPUs and cost Intel about 475 million USD; modern solvers catch such errors before tape-out",
        "mis": "people assume NP-hardness means SAT is useless in practice. CDCL solvers handle industrial instances with millions of variables in seconds, vastly outperforming theoretical worst-case bounds on structured inputs. Another myth is that all SAT solvers are similar; performance gaps of 1000x or more across solvers on the same instance are common",
    }

