    T["the development of perspective in Western painting"] = {
        "_cat": "arts_and_humanities",
        "what": "the technique, codified in early-15th-century Florence, of representing three-dimensional space on a flat surface using mathematical rules so that parallel lines converge to vanishing points, transforming Western painting's depiction of space",
        "how": "Filippo Brunelleschi performed an experiment around 1413 painting the Florence Baptistery with a mirror and viewing peephole, demonstrating geometric perspective. Leon Battista Alberti's 1435 treatise On Painting formalized the construction: a horizon line, vanishing points, and a grid of orthogonals constructed from a fixed viewpoint. Painters from Masaccio onward used these rules to position figures convincingly in space",
        "why": "perspective changed how Europeans saw images and arguably how they understood space itself. Combined with the printing press, it allowed widespread study of optics, architecture, and engineering. Albrecht Durer, Piero della Francesca, and others produced manuals that fed Renaissance and Baroque art, and the same geometry underlies modern computer graphics and photography",
        "vs": "linear perspective differs from atmospheric perspective (using color and contrast for depth, used by Leonardo and Chinese landscape painters), from isometric/axonometric projection (used in Chinese, Japanese, and modern engineering drawings), and from medieval European depictions, which conveyed importance through scale and position rather than geometric viewpoint",
        "ex": "Masaccio's Trinity fresco (c. 1427) in Santa Maria Novella in Florence is one of the earliest masterworks of single-point perspective. The barrel-vaulted chapel appears to recede into the wall convincingly enough that contemporary viewers reportedly thought a real chapel had been opened in the church",
        "mis": "people think perspective is the only correct way to depict space. It is one cultural and historical convention, not a universal truth; East Asian landscape traditions developed equally sophisticated but different conventions. Another myth is that perspective was unknown before Brunelleschi; Greco-Roman wall paintings show empirical attempts, but the systematic geometric method was a Renaissance achievement",
    }

